[{:seon.task/name "a-point-is-found-without-walking-the-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.ctx.wire/at-t` finds branch db's commit holding t by walking the line back from db, a ``datahike.api/commit-as-db`` a commit, on every call; `seon.root/back-point` calls it and keeps the commit when `(= t (:max-tx at))`. Measured 2026-11-11 (LMDB, Seon's layout, one-datom commits, the point at the first of n): 379k, 4.17M and 42.1M engine ticks, 1.7, 18 and 186 MB, at n = 10, 100 and 1,000 (42k ticks and 185 KB a commit). What replaces it: ``datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx`` (Datahike pin `15e5e636`, docs/storage.md#a-points-commit): `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` answers `{:branch :commit :t}`, the commit of db's lineage whose last transaction t' is the greatest <= t, exact when `(= t (:t p))`; `(datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)` the commit whose span holds t, which is what at-t answers now (its earliest commit holding t). Both read each commit once per process and keep it in memory: measured over the same span, a first lookup 169k, 1.69M and 16.9M ticks (one commit-record read a commit walked), a repeat 2.0k, 20k and 205k with no read, k reads after k new commits. Change: back-point takes `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` and keeps `:commit` when `(= t (:t p))`, else refuses as now; at-t becomes `(d/commit-as-db (:store db) (:commit (datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)))`, and where a sweep took the commit the refusal `{:type :commit-expired :branch :tx}` (ex-info at its named edge) gives `(d/as-of db t)` as now; `:tx-after-head` and `:tx-before-first-commit` are a caller's error, never a nearby commit. Delete the walk loop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-point-reads-each-commit-once}}]
